President Trump is facing sharp  criticism for granting a pardon to Joe  Arpaio the former sheriff of Maricopa  County Arizona Arpaio gained notoriety  for his aggressive action to arrest  illegal immigrants the pardon was  granted just hours before hurricane  Harvey made landfall lawmakers and civil  rights advocates are questioning the  president's decision and its timing to  clear up Arpaio of a criminal contempt  charge and possible jail time  Arpaio was scheduled to be sentenced in  October  Errol Barnett is at the White House with  more this morning Erol good morning good  morning while the pardon itself comes as  no surprise Democrat and Republican  senators as well as a few civil rights  organizations are all slamming president  Trump's decision one group going as far  as to call the move a defense of racism  but Sheriff Joe can feel good president  Trump alluded to his impending pardon at  a campaign star rally in Phoenix on  Tuesday do the people in this room like  Sheriff Joe was Sheriff Joe convicted  for doing his job  Arpaio who called himself America's  toughest sheriff was convicted of  criminal contempt last month for defying  a judge's order to stop detaining people  solely on the suspicion that they were  in the country illegally it was a  practice that was widely criticized as  racially profiling Latinos in a White  House statement President Trump  applauded Arpaio for quote his life's  work protecting the public from the  scourges of crime and illegal  immigration and said after more than 50  years of admirable service to our nation  he is worthy of a presidential pardon  CBS News has learned that pardon was  driven by the White House and did not  follow typical Department of Justice  guidelines it requires at least a  five-year threshold before the agency  can review a clemency petition the  Department of Justice has said the  president exercised his lawful authority  and we respect his decision the former  sheriff thanked the President and said  he will hold a news conference next week  to tell his side of the story pardon or  no pardon I'll be with him to the  and I say that I'll have more to speak  out and that the true story come out  about the abuse of the judicial system  and politics the decision has outraged  civil rights organizations like the ACLU  it called the pardon and endorsement of  racism and that by pardoning Arpaio  quote Trump has chosen lawlessness over  justice division over unity hurt over  healing now Arizona's Republican  senators John McCain and Jeff Flake have  both denounced president Trump's  decision and all of this happened while  staff at the White House for dealing  with yet another staff shake-up it was  announced on Friday the president's  deputy assistant Sebastian Gorka is no  longer with the administration  one official explaining that Gawker did  not resign Dana  another busy night at the White House  Errol Barnett thank you
 

